= Purpose

This document states the *assumptions of the simulation* of the Rail-Across-Suite: what it reads of a project in rail-across-1.0, how it works out runs, occupancies and capacities from it, and what it simplifies on the way. It complements the format description rail-across-1.0: that one says what is stored, this one what the tools compute from it.

Four tools compute:

- the *Fahrplan-Editor* and the *Simulation-Tool*: each train's run, its times and what it occupies;
- the *Kapazitäts-Sim*: the capacity of stretches and stations after UIC 406;
- the *Baubetriebs-Sim*: which tracks and switches are in service at a construction stage (it runs no trains).

The simulation follows five principles:

- *OSRD's physics.* The running time calculation is the envelope simulation of OSRD, the Open Source Railway Designer of the OpenRail Association, ported to Rust (`sim/`, at OSRD `fda69ee1`). What OSRD computes, the suite computes the same way, with one exception: braking depends on the grade. What is not ported is listed under "Limits".
- *The infrastructure as designed.* Grades, curves and speeds come from the tracks' design values, not from drawn lines. Where data is missing, the simulation runs with a neutral value (level, no limit) and *reports the gap* instead of inventing something.
- *Each train for itself first, then all together.* Each train is first run alone. Its run gives its blocking times; only the dispatching brings the trains together, and runs a train again where it has to wait or is sent another way.
- *Determined, not random.* The same inputs give the same results. There are no random variables and no spread.
- *SI units.* Metres, seconds, m/s, kg, N; grades in ‰ (m/km), rising positive in the direction of travel. Clock times are seconds from midnight of the timetable day. The format's speeds in km/h are converted to m/s on reading.

= From the timetable to the occupancy

A train goes through nine steps. The sections of this document follow them.

+ *Places:* the train's points are matched to operational points and track ends of the net.
+ *Way:* the way over the net through all points.
+ *Properties of the way:* length, grades (with the curves) and permitted speeds along it.
+ *Train:* vehicle and traction mode.
+ *Running time calculation:* the fastest run, the run with the margin, and the run the timetable keeps.
+ *Clock:* the run in clock time, with the departures the train waits for.
+ *Blocks and blocking times:* what the train holds, and when.
+ *Dispatching:* show the conflicts, or give the trains the net one after the other.
+ *Capacity:* the blocking times compressed after UIC 406.

= Places: stations, halts and track ends

A train's point names an operational point (`operationalPointId`) or a track end (`endMarkId`) by its id. The simulation makes places of them:

#tbl((22%, 1fr),
  [Place], [What belongs to it],
  [Station], [an operational point's station tracks (`trackType` `station`) with the platforms on them; where an operational point has no station tracks but stopping places, the tracks of its stopping places and platforms, each only as far as they reach],
  [Halt], [an operational point with platforms on the line's tracks only, no tracks of its own],
  [Track end], [a buffer stop or a boundary of the planning area, a place at the end of a track],
)

These rules apply:

- A train *begins and ends* at a station or a track end only. A halt is no beginning and no end; a track end never lies on the way.
- *A train stops at a stopping place only* (`stoppingPlaces`), on the track asked for where a track is asked for. This holds for its start and its end too.
  - A *passenger train* stops only at a stopping place at a platform: one a platform names (`stoppingPlaceIds`), one marked a passenger stop (`passengerStop`), or one along which a platform lies on its track.
  - A *train without passengers* stops at any stopping place. These are the kinds Gz, Lz and Dz and the freight kinds GC, KLV, EK and DGS; every other kind counts as a passenger train.
  - Where a station has no such stopping place (on the track asked for), the train is not run and the point says so.
- *Where in the stopping place:* 10 m before its end, seen in the direction of travel. A stopping place shorter than 20 m is stopped at in its middle. At a start, "the end" is the one the train leaves towards. The direction follows from the way, so each stopping place gives two candidates, one per direction. With several candidates, the one reached the shortest way is taken; at the start, the one with the shortest first leg.
- *Where it only passes,* any track of the station will do.
- *The train's head is what is computed.* A stopping train stands with its head at that point. Whether its rear reaches beyond the stopping place or the platform is not checked.
- *A pass* is timed at the middle of what the station covers along the way.
- *At a boundary of the planning area* a train runs in at the boundary's speed and out at most at it. Where the boundary has no speed, what the line and the train allow there applies. At a buffer stop it stands.

= The way over the net

- *Shortest way.* The way from point to point is the shortest by length. It is searched after Dijkstra over pairs of track and direction, on over switches along their routes, links and joints. The cost is the metres run. Diverging, switches and speeds cost nothing more.
- *No reversing, unless asked for.* From a stop on, the train runs on in the direction it arrived in.
- *Reversal (Richtungswechsel).* A point on the way can ask for one (`reverse`). The train stops there, stands its turnaround time (`turnaround`, 300 s where none is given; its dwell does not count) and runs on in the other direction. Its new head is where its tail stood, one train length back along the way it came in. The way therefore jumps there, and the stretch the train stood on is not run again. Its first and last point cannot reverse. The running time calculation sees the whole run as one way. For the first train length after the reversal, the grade under the train and the limits its tail must clear are therefore those of the stretch as the train came in on it. The speeds the signals signal are found on each part for itself.
- *Several tracks or stopping places of a station* are tried in turn, the one reached the shortest way first. Another is taken only where no way leads on from the first. At most 200 legs are tried.
- *Preferred itinerary.* Where a point names an itinerary of the signalling (`itineraryId`), the train runs along its tracks, where the variant has it and it leads there. Else it runs the shortest way and says so.
- *Sent another way by the dispatching.* Where the dispatching sends a train over another route (see "Dispatching"), it runs to that route's start signal, along its tracks to the target signal, and on from there the shortest way.

= Grades

== From the heights

- A track's height runs *straight between its height points*. *Vertical curves* do not enter the grade: at a height point the grade jumps from one tangent to the other.
- The grade of a piece between two cuts is `(z(b) − z(a)) / (b − a) · 1000` in ‰, signed by the direction of travel. The cuts are the height points, the element ends and the ends of the way's pieces.
- *A track without heights* (fewer than two height points) is taken as level and reported as a gap.

== Curves as a grade

The resistance of a curve is added to the grade as an *equivalent grade*, after *Röckl*'s formula for standard gauge:

#tbl((30%, 1fr),
  [Radius r], [Curve resistance w in ‰],
  [r ≥ 300 m], [`650 / (r − 55)`],
  [30 m \< r \< 300 m], [`500 / (r − 30)`],
  [r ≤ 30 m, straight], [0],
)

- An *arc* has its resistance over its whole length.
- A *transition* is cut into pieces of at most 10 m, each with the radius at its middle. The curvature runs from `1/r1` to `1/r2` linearly on a clothoid, as `3t² − 2t³` on a Bloss curve.
- The resistance counts as a rise *in both directions*: on a fall it lessens the effective fall.
- Cant plays no part in the resistance. The vehicle's gauge and wheelbase do not enter.
- Switches have their curve resistance as far as their branch is stored as a track with arc elements.
- The sum is rounded to 0.001 ‰; equal neighbouring values are merged.

*Example:* an arc of r = 500 m on a rise of 4 ‰ gives 4 + 650 / 445 = 5.461 ‰.

== Averaged under the train

The running time calculation takes not the grade under the head but the *mean grade under the whole train*, from its tail to its head. The train is taken as a band of mass spread evenly over its length. Before the start of the way, the tail is cut off at the way's beginning. The force of the grade is `−m · g · sin(arctan(i / 1000))` with g = 9.81 m/s².

= Speeds

== What the line permits

- *Design speed:* the speed of an alignment element (`speed`). An element without a speed sets no limit, and the piece is reported as a gap, unless a signal signals a speed there.
- *Signalled speed:* for each train route the way runs along, the speed a logical signal at its start signal signals for it (`signallingSpeeds`) holds from the start to the target signal. For that, start and target signal stand on the way, the route's tracks follow each other in the way, and the start signal acts in the direction of travel. Where the route starts at a virtual signal at a boundary of the planning area (at most 20 m from it), the boundary's speed holds.
- *Lf 7:* where a speed board Lf 7 stands on the route, acting in the direction of travel or both, the route's speed ends at it. From there the speed it shows holds up to the next Lf 7 or the target signal (symbol `7` = 70 km/h).
- *Precedence:* where a signal signals a speed, it *holds instead of the design speed*, even where it is higher. The design speed holds only where no signal speaks.
- *Switch speeds* do not enter directly. They reach the run through the route's signalled speed, which the Signalling-Tool proposes from them.
- *Vehicle:* the vehicle's top speed holds everywhere.
- *Leaving over a boundary:* the way's last metre carries the boundary's speed.

== How the train follows them

- All limits make the *most restrictive speed profile* (MRSP).
- *A limit holds until the train's tail has left it.* Behind a speed restriction, the train accelerates only once it has left it with its whole length.
- *Before a lower limit the train brakes* so that its head reaches it at its beginning. The braking curve depends on the grade (see "The running time calculation").
- The driver is *ideal*: they brake at the last possible moment, accelerate with full effort and have no reaction time. Announcements (Lf 6, Zs 3v, distant signals) need no effect of their own: where braking begins follows from the physics.
- *Entry speed* at a boundary: the boundary's, at most the train's top speed, and at most so fast that it can still brake down to every limit along the way: `√(v² + 2 · 0.8 · γ · s)` with γ the vehicle's braking deceleration and s the distance to the limit. The factor 0.8 leaves room for a fall.
- The speed profile shown is the one without the train's length.

= The train

== Vehicle

A vehicle is rolling stock in OSRD's RailJSON. The simulation reads of it:

#tbl((30%, 1fr),
  [Value], [Meaning],
  [`mass`], [mass in kg],
  [`length`], [length in m (for averaging the grade, for the limits and for clearing the blocks)],
  [`max_speed`], [top speed in m/s],
  [`inertia_coefficient`], [mass factor ρ for the rotating masses, else 1.05],
  [`rolling_resistance`], [resistance to movement after Davis: `A + B · v + C · v²` in N, v in m/s],
  [`const_gamma`], [service braking deceleration on the level in m/s²],
  [`effort_curves`], [tractive effort over speed, per traction mode and comfort],
)

- *Tractive effort curve:* straight between its points. Below the first point its effort holds, above the last point likewise. The curve taken is the one for the train's comfort, else the one for the standard comfort, else the mode's default curve.
- *One vehicle per train.* What is computed is a formation of exactly one traction unit. Formations of several vehicles, a hauled load, brake position and braked weight percentage are stored but not computed. A train with such a formation cannot be run yet.
- A resistance to movement whose coefficients are all below one is read as N/kg on import and multiplied by the mass. OSRD's example vehicles state it so.
- No adhesion: the tractive effort is not limited by the friction between wheel and rail. Auxiliary loads and the contact line's power limits do not count.

== Traction mode

- The traction mode is the train's own, where it names one.
- Else, where the electrification is planned: the vehicle's electric mode whose voltage has wire *the whole way*; else its thermal one; else the vehicle's default. Where the net has no contact wire planned at all, every vehicle runs in its default.
- *One mode for the whole way.* There is no change on the way. Where a train runs electric over a stretch without a matching wire, it runs there all the same, and the stretches are reported.
- Under wire lies what a contact wire's suspension points and section insulators carry. The wire's voltage must equal the mode's name (`15000V`).

= The running time calculation

== Equation of motion

The train is a point mass at its head, on which three forces act: the tractive effort F, the resistance to movement R(v) and the force of the grade G from the mean grade with the curves. Its acceleration is

```
a = (F + G − R(v)) / (m · ρ)
```

- *Time step* 2 s. A step's mean acceleration is estimated after Runge-Kutta, then a Newton step `Δx = v · Δt + a · Δt² / 2` follows. A step that would fall below zero is shortened.
- *Accelerating:* full tractive effort.
- *Holding:* just as much effort as resistance and grade ask for. Where full effort is not enough, the train slows down and catches up afterwards.
- *Coasting:* no tractive effort.
- *Braking:* at the vehicle's deceleration γ on the level, *plus the grade under the train*: `a = −γ + G / (m · ρ)`, with G the weight force as in accelerating (averaged under the train). A rise shortens the braking distance, a fall lengthens it: at 0.6 m/s² from 100 km/h the train needs about 643 m on the level and about 760 m on a fall of 10 ‰. On any fall, at least a tenth of γ is left. The deceleration does not depend on the speed. OSRD's constant braking, which ignores the grade, stays selectable in the calculation (`braking` `constant`); the suite does not use it.
- At a stand the train stays standing as long as effort and grade do not overcome the resistance. Where a train stalls on a rise, the calculation stops with `impossible_simulation` and the position.

== The three runs

#tbl((18%, 1fr),
  [Run], [What it is],
  [`base`], [the fastest run: accelerate with full effort, hold the limit, brake at the last moment],
  [`provisional`], [the run with the train's standard margin],
  [`final`], [the run that also keeps given arrival times; the timetable takes this one],
)

The suite gives the calculation no arrival times, only stops with their dwell. `final` is therefore the run with the margin. The timetable's departures act only afterwards (see "Clock").

== Margin

- A train has a running time margin: in percent of the running time (`5 %`), in minutes per 100 km (`4 min/100km`), or none. A new train has 5 % after MARECO.
- The margin holds *for the whole way* as one range. The sections between the stops are computed each for itself.
- *MARECO* (the default) spreads the margin saving energy: the top speed is capped, and before braking the train coasts. Cap and start of coasting are searched by bisection until the target time is met. Below 1 m/s the train is not slowed down.
- *Linear* slows down all speeds by the same ratio.

== Stops

- A train stops at every point that asks for a stop or a reversal, and always at its end, unless it leaves over a boundary there. It brakes to zero, stands its dwell (`dwell`, 60 s for a new point) or, where it reverses, its turnaround time (300 s where none is given), and starts again with full effort.
- Two stops at the same position become one, with the longer dwell.
- A point's least dwell (`leastDwell`) is stored but not computed yet.
- The calculation gives the mechanical energy at the wheel, without recuperation and without efficiencies.

= Clock

- A train leaves at the departure of its first point.
- *Departure at a point:* where the train would leave earlier, it waits for it. The rest of its run moves by the wait. Nothing is computed again for that, as a longer stop does not change the run.
- Every place along the way has an arrival and a departure. For a pass, both are the same.
- Clock times past 24:00 belong to the next day and are not wrapped.

= Signals

- *A main signal* is a real signal with a logical signal that is no distant signal, distant repeater and no shunting signal. Only main signals bound blocks.
- *Announcing* is a real signal with a distant signal, distant repeater or multi-section signal (Ks). It decides where a train asks for its next block.
- A signal acts in its direction (`at.direction`, without one in the direction of the stationing) or in both. It may stand at several places (`alsoAt`).
- *Signals do not steer the run by their aspects.* The simulation sets no aspects (Hp 0, Ks 2) and models no driver behaviour at signals. A signal acts on the run in two ways only: by the speed it signals, and by the dispatching, which lets a train wait before it.
- *Waiting at a signal* is a planned stop a few metres before it. The train brakes as before any stop, not from the distant signal on, and starts again with full effort after the wait.
- *Virtual signals* at boundaries of the planning area begin or end routes without a real signal.
- An announcing signal's *sight distance* is `sight.required`, else `sight.minimum`.

= Blocks

- *A block* is the stretch of a train's way from one main signal acting in its direction of travel to the next. The first block begins at the start, the last ends at the end. Without main signals the whole way is one block.
- *Overlap:* a block reaches beyond its end signal by the longest of the overlaps stored for that signal. The simulation does not work out their length itself. The catalogue after Ril 301 proposes it in the Signalling-Tool: 50 m up to 40 km/h, 100 m up to 60 km/h, 200 m above; longer on a fall steeper than 5 ‰ (`L = L0 · 0.75 / (0.75 − 9.81 · |i| / 1000)`, rounded up to 10 m). This table is a draft, not checked against the Ril 819.0202 in force.
- *Track detection:* where the variant has track sections, a block holds every section it touches by more than 0.5 m. The sections' extent follows from their detection points over the net. Stretches no section covers the block holds as stretches of track.
- *Conflict:* two trains conflict where they hold the same section at the same time, or stretches of the same track that overlap by more than 0.5 m.
- *Route times:* a block's setting and release time are those of the train route from its signal to the next, else the layer's (`routeTimes`), else the timetable's (`blockingTimes`), else 12 s and 6 s.

= Blocking times

A train holds a block from the moment it must find it free until the block is released after it has run through. This follows the blocking time after Ril 405 and OSRD's spacing requirements:

#tbl((26%, 1fr),
  [Part], [How it is computed],
  [Route setting time], [before asking; 12 s where nothing else applies],
  [Approach], [from the point where the train sees the announcing signal: its sight distance before the first announcing signal since the main signal before; without an announcing signal, 1000 m before the block's signal],
  [Running in the block], [as computed, with margin and stops],
  [Clearing], [until the train's tail has passed the block's end signal (end signal + train length)],
  [Release time], [after that; 6 s where nothing else applies],
)

- The *approach time* is that of the train's actual run. A sight time in seconds (`sight` in `blockingTimes`) is stored but not computed yet.
- *First block:* from the departure, less the setting time. *Stop in the approach:* where the train stands within the approach, it asks for the block only as it leaves there.
- *Last block:* until the arrival at the end and the release time.
- *Reversal:* the blocks are cut there as at an end and a start. The block before ends at the reversal point, without an end signal and without an overlap. It is held until the train, running the other way, has left the stretch it stood on (one train length), plus the release time. The block after begins there without a signal and is asked for as the train leaves, less the setting time. A train held there waits at the reversal point and stands longer.
- *Sectional release:* with track sections, each section is released as soon as the train's tail has left it, plus the release time. A route then waits only for the section another still holds.
- *Overlap:* its sections are released with the block. Where the train comes to a stand within 200 m before the end signal, they are released a while after it stops: after the overlap's release delay (`releaseDelay`), else after 60 s.

= Dispatching

A timetable says what happens where trains meet (`dispatching.mode`):

- *`show`* runs every train for itself and shows the conflicts, as OSRD does.
- *`wait`* (the default) sets the trains' routes in order of time, as an interlocking does: who asks first gets it first. Where two ask at the same moment, the departure or the order of the kinds of trains decides (`priority`), then the train number. The default order of the kinds is ICE, EC, IC, IRE, RE, RB, S, Dz, Gz, Lz.

In mode `wait`:

- A train asks for its next block as it nears the block's signal, at the beginning of the blocking time. It gets it where none of its sections is held by the blocks others have been given already.
- Of another train's last block, while it does not have its next yet, what it would still hold if it came to a stand before the next signal counts as held. The overlap counts as held only until a while after that stop.
- *Held:* the train takes another train route from the same signal that is free and still leads to where it runs, into the other track of a station to cross there, say. Where there is none, it waits for the route that is freed first: longer at its last stop between the signal before and the block's, as far as that lies within the approach (its start among them, where it then leaves later); else 10 m before the signal. For its first block it waits at its start.
- A train that waits or is sent another way is *run again*: the wait is a stop, the other way another path.
- *Limits:* the dispatching makes at most 80 decisions per train (for all trains together, plus 50), and a train asks for a block again at most 20 times. Beyond that the remaining trains run as they would, and what is left is reported as a conflict.
- *Delay* is how much later a train arrives than run alone.
- *Headway at a boundary:* where a boundary of the planning area has a least headway (`headway`), a train entering there sooner after the one before is reported. It is not moved for it.
- The dispatching does not optimise. It changes no order beyond "who asks first" and keeps no connections and turnarounds.

= Capacity after UIC 406

- *Stretches:* a stretch runs from one node to the next along the trains' ways, the same for both directions. A node is a station of at least two tracks, or a place where a train begins or ends.
- *A stretch's trains* are those that run from one of its places to the other, with their time at its beginning and the blocks that reach more than 1 m into it.
- *Compression:* the trains whose time at the stretch's beginning lies in the period `[from, to)` are laid, in the order they run there, as early behind those before as their blocking times let them, never before the train before. A section or stretch of track a block releases before its end counts only until then.
- *Occupancy time:* from the first block begun to the last block ended of the compressed trains. The *occupancy rate* is its share of the period, with and without additional times (per train and fixed).
- *Recommended bounds* after UIC 406 (2013): for a period of up to two hours the peak hour's value holds, else the day's.

#tbl((34%, 22%, 1fr),
  [Kind of line], [Peak hour], [Day],
  [Suburban], [85 %], [70 %],
  [High speed], [75 %], [60 %],
  [Mixed traffic], [75 %], [60 %],
)

- *Minimum headway:* for every two trains that hold something the same, in both orders, how soon after the first the second may be at the beginning without their blocking times overlapping. The values are summed up by kinds of trains.
- *Binding elements:* for each train of the critical chain after the first, the element where its blocking time touches that of the train it is bound to: of all that the two both hold, the one where it could come least earlier. It is shown with the block of each of the two trains and the moment one releases it and the other takes it, as compressed.
- *Conflict areas:* what two or more trains of the period hold: a track section, or a stretch of track where the stretches they hold overlap by more than 0.5 m, taken as one. Each area has the trains that hold it and the union of the times it is held. The Kapazitäts-Sim draws the areas and the binding elements on a schematic plan of the variant.
- *Stations:* for every track and section of a station, the union of the times it is held and its share of the period.
- *Construction stages:* the capacity is computed on a variant's net at a construction stage: the existing state, every day something is put into or taken out of service, or the final state. The state at a stage follows from the lifespans; another variant or stage can be laid beside it.

= Construction scenarios

A construction scenario of a timetable (decision 0030) closes stretches of track for a while, takes trains out and diverts trains by hand. The Baubetriebs-Sim runs the timetable in it for one day:

- *The net of the day* is the variant at the stage of that day, as the lifespans of the Layout-Tool make it.
- *A closure* holds from its first to its last day, all day or in a daily window (possession). A window whose end lies before its beginning runs past midnight into the next day; a closure of the day before therefore reaches into the early hours of the day.
- *No way runs over a closed stretch*, not by more than 0.5 m. The rest of the track stays usable: a train may stop in front of the closure or start behind it. This holds for the shortest way, for a preferred itinerary and for a route the dispatching sends a train over.
- *A train is touched* by a closure where its way, as it runs in the timetable, runs over the closed stretch while the closure holds: from its head entering the stretch to its tail leaving it. A train not touched runs as it is.
- *A touched train is diverted:* it runs the shortest way with what touches it closed, keeping its points. Where the way around touches another closure, that one is closed for it too, three times at the most.
- *By hand:* a diversion set by hand runs the train on its own points — its way, its stops, its itineraries — and comes before the computed one; what it touches is closed for it as for any.
- *Not runnable:* a train for which no way is left says at which point it found none. A point on a closed stretch, or one that can only be reached over it, cannot be kept; a diversion by hand without it can.
- *Taken out:* a train taken out does not run and holds nothing.
- *Dispatching:* the trains that run are given the net as in the Fahrplan-Editor (see "Dispatching"), each on its net with what is closed for it. A closure in a window does not close a track for the trains outside it.
- *Beside the timetable:* each train is set beside its regular run on the same net of the day: arrival, running time and delay, waits and conflicts.

= Limits

Not computed, partly because parts of OSRD are not ported, partly because the suite does not use them yet:

- ETCS and LZB braking curves, driver behaviour at signals, aspects, the signalling simulation;
- a change of traction mode on the way, electrical profiles, power restrictions, neutral sections;
- formations of several vehicles, hauled load, brake position and braked weight percentage;
- vertical curves in the grade, a braking deceleration depending on the speed, adhesion;
- connections, turnarounds, delay scenarios and a study's stochastic runs (`runs`, `seed`) — they are stored;
- the least dwell (`leastDwell`) and the sight time (`sight`);
- given arrival times in the running time calculation.

